<p>Gentle walking is usually fine within a few days of a hair transplant. The gym, running and heavy lifting generally wait around three to four weeks, swimming around four, and contact sport or anything played in a helmet until a review has cleared it. Smoking and alcohol are worth stopping before the procedure as well as after it, because nicotine narrows the small blood vessels that new grafts depend on, and alcohol increases bleeding and swelling.</p>

<p>Those are general ranges, consistent with the <a href="/hair-transplant-aftercare">hair transplant aftercare guidance</a>. Protocols differ between clinics, and your own written instructions, and the clearance you are given at your reviews, take priority over anything here. What follows are the reasons behind each wait, because a rule is easier to keep when you know what it protects.</p>

<h2 id="why-activity-is-restricted">Why activity is restricted at all</h2>

<p>For the first few days, grafts sit in shallow incisions held by little more than early clotting, and over roughly ten days they become firmly anchored. During that window, and for a while after it, four separate things can cause problems:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pressure.</strong> Straining, heavy lifting and bending at the waist raise blood pressure and pressure in the scalp. Early on, that can restart bleeding, worsen swelling and, at worst, push a graft out.</li>
  <li><strong>Sweat.</strong> Sweat does not lift grafts by itself, but it softens crusts before they are ready, makes the scalp itch, and tempts you to wipe or scratch, which does move things.</li>
  <li><strong>Impact and friction.</strong> A ball, a knock, or a helmet dragged across the hairline can dislodge grafts in the early weeks and irritate healing skin after that.</li>
  <li><strong>Water and sun.</strong> Pools, the sea and lakes expose small healing wounds to chlorine, salt and bacteria, and the new skin over the recipient area burns easily for several weeks.</li>
</ul>

<p>Each activity is restricted for as long as the problem it causes lasts, which is why there is no single date for everything. <a href="/blog/first-14-days-after-a-hair-transplant">The first 14 days after a hair transplant</a> describes what the scalp looks like day by day across the most fragile part of that window.</p>

<h2 id="a-staged-return-to-exercise">A staged return to exercise</h2>

