<p>Treat these as bands, not appointments. A review can move any of them either way, depending on how your scalp has healed, the size of the session and the technique used.</p>

<h3>Coming back gradually</h3>

<p>When the gym is cleared, start lighter than usual. Lower weights, shorter sessions and a towel kept off the scalp let you see how it responds. Keep exercises that put the head below the heart, such as decline benches and inversions, for a little later, and stop if you feel throbbing or tightness in the scalp. Saunas and steam rooms combine heat and sweat, so ask before using them; they are usually treated like the gym, or later.</p>

<h3>After a strip (FUT) procedure</h3>

<p>A strip harvest leaves a closed wound across the back of the head. Exercises that flex or strain the neck, such as heavy squats, deadlifts and some shoulder work, pull on that wound, and a scar stretched while it heals can widen. Restrictions on those lifts are often longer after FUT than after FUE, so ask specifically if you had a strip procedure.</p>

<h2 id="helmets-and-contact-sport">Helmets, caps and contact sport</h2>

<p>Helmets are the hardest restriction to live with for anyone who depends on a two-wheeler. A helmet's rim drags across the hairline as it goes on, its padding presses on the grafts at the front and the donor area at the back, and it traps heat and sweat against both. Plan on roughly the first two weeks without riding, as rider or pillion, and wear a helmet again only once a review has cleared it. When you do, choose one that sits loosely, lower it straight down rather than pulling it forward, and take it off as soon as you arrive. Riding without a helmet is not an alternative: it is illegal, and a fall on a healing scalp is far worse than a delayed return to the road.</p>

<p>Loose caps are usually allowed sooner than helmets, commonly from around ten days once the clinic agrees, but tight caps and anything that grips should wait. Sport played in a helmet, such as cricket or cycling, carries the same problem with impact added. Contact sport, including football with heading, wrestling, kabaddi and martial arts, risks a direct blow to the grafts or the donor area. Both wait until a review clears them, which is usually later than the gym. If your sport involves headgear, raise it at the consultation rather than at your first review.</p>

<h2 id="smoking-before-and-after">Smoking before and after a hair transplant</h2>

<p>Smoking is the habit that most clearly affects healing. Nicotine narrows small blood vessels, and the carbon monoxide in smoke reduces how much oxygen the blood carries. A newly placed graft has no blood supply of its own for its first days; it survives on what reaches it from the surrounding skin until new vessels grow in. Anything that reduces blood flow in the scalp during that window works against it. A recent review of complications after follicular unit extraction names smoking, alongside diabetes and high blood pressure, among the conditions that impair scalp circulation and the healing of the recipient area, and suggests stopping for at least a month before surgery (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12909172/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">review of FUE complications, Frontiers in Medicine</a>). Smoking also slows the healing of the donor sites.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Before.</strong> Stopping several weeks beforehand is better than stopping a few days beforehand, and a few days is better than not stopping. NHS hospital advice on stopping before an operation makes the same point: the earlier you stop, the more you gain, but stopping at any point before surgery still helps.</li>
  <li><strong>After.</strong> Stay off cigarettes at least until the clinic clears you, while the grafts establish their blood supply and the donor sites close. Many people use the procedure as the moment to stop for good.</li>
  <li><strong>Other forms of nicotine.</strong> Bidis, hookah, chewing tobacco, gutka and nicotine vapes all deliver nicotine. Whether nicotine replacement such as patches or gum is acceptable during recovery is a question for the doctor, because advice on it differs.</li>
</ul>

<p>Smoking is also one of the commonly cited reasons for a slower course of growth, as <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-growth-timeline-month-by-month">the month-by-month growth timeline</a> notes. Stopping is hard, so tell the doctor at the consultation that you smoke. It is far better planned around honestly than discovered on the day.</p>

<h2 id="alcohol-before-and-after">Alcohol before and after</h2>

<p>Alcohol matters for three reasons. It increases bleeding, and the same review lists it alongside anti-inflammatory painkillers and some supplements as a bleeding risk. It adds to swelling and dehydration in the first days, when forehead swelling is already common. And some medicines prescribed after surgery should not be taken with alcohol.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Before.</strong> Clinics usually ask you to avoid alcohol for a few days beforehand. Follow the interval you are given.</li>
  <li><strong>After.</strong> Avoid it for at least the first several days, and for as long as you are taking a medicine that should not be mixed with it. Ask at your first review before drinking again.</li>
  <li><strong>Weddings and parties.</strong> If an occasion falls in the first week, sit out the drinks or move the procedure date. A late night also makes it harder to sleep propped up and keep your head off the pillow.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you take a blood thinner or have high blood pressure, alcohol needs extra care around the procedure; <a href="/blog/hair-transplant-with-diabetes-or-high-blood-pressure">hair transplant with diabetes or high blood pressure</a> covers the planning those conditions need.</p>

